\section{Introduction}

When attention layers are added to three different recurrent architectures, their LAMBADA performance becomes near-identical: cross-architecture standard deviation drops from 0.024 to 0.005, a 79\% reduction. Yet this aggregate convergence hides an asymmetry at the per-example level. The three architectures reach similar overall accuracy by helping and hurting almost entirely different examples, with pairwise Jaccard similarity below 0.10. Two models can agree on a benchmark score while disagreeing on which inputs they get right. This tension between aggregate agreement and per-example disagreement arises in the context of a rapid architectural shift toward hybrid recurrent-attention models, which interleave attention layers within linear recurrent backbones. Jamba \citep{lieber2024jamba} combines Mamba layers with Transformer blocks; Griffin \citep{de2024griffin} mixes gated linear recurrences with local attention; RWKV-6 \citep{peng2024eagle} integrates matrix-valued states with attention-like mechanisms; RecurrentGemma \citep{botev2024recurrentgemma} pairs linear recurrences with sliding-window attention. These systems now serve as production-grade alternatives to pure Transformers, and the practical question of how much attention to include is typically answered by comparing aggregate benchmark numbers. \citet{wang2025hybrid} provide a controlled model zoo spanning three recurrent architectures, five attention ratios, and two parameter scales, which enables systematic study of how attention allocation affects these hybrid designs.

Most evaluations of hybrid architectures compare aggregate benchmark scores and report that a given attention ratio achieves a certain accuracy averaged across tasks or averaged across architectures. This practice can mask substantial per-example behavioral diversity. \citet{damour2022underspecification} showed that models with equivalent held-out performance can disagree on individual predictions, a phenomenon they termed underspecification. In their framing, a training pipeline is underspecified when it admits multiple models that perform equally on the training objective but differ on individual test inputs. \citet{mccoy2020berts} demonstrated a concrete instance: BERT models trained from different random seeds produce similar aggregate scores but diverge on specific linguistic constructions. Both studies examined variation from random initialization or training noise, not from architectural choice. Whether the gap between aggregate and per-example behavior extends to hybrid recurrent architectures, where the source of variation is the recurrent backbone itself, remains unquantified. The question matters because practitioners increasingly choose between recurrent backbones based on aggregate benchmarks; if architectures that score identically in aggregate behave differently on individual inputs, such choices carry hidden consequences for deployment reliability and ensemble construction.

We present the first systematic per-example analysis of how attention affects cross-architecture agreement in hybrid recurrent models. Using the model zoo of \citet{wang2025hybrid}, we compare three architectures (GatedDeltaNet, RetNet, HGRN2) at five attention ratios (0\% to 25\%) and two scales (340M and 1.3B parameters). These three architectures represent distinct approaches to linear recurrence: gated delta rules, multi-scale exponential decay, and gated state expansion. We evaluate all 30 configurations on 10 benchmarks spanning retrieval, comprehension, reasoning, and mixed task categories. On LAMBADA, we conduct a full per-example decomposition across 5{,}153 examples, where each example is classified as helped, hurt, or unchanged when attention is added to each architecture. We then measure cross-architecture overlap in these per-example effect sets using Jaccard similarity. Figure~\ref{fig:decoupling} previews our central result: aggregate convergence and per-example divergence follow different trajectories as attention ratio increases.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig1_decoupling_hero.pdf}
\caption{Aggregate convergence and per-example divergence are decoupled. (a) Cross-architecture standard deviation on LAMBADA decreases gradually with attention ratio at 1.3B (solid) but partially rebounds at 340M (dashed). (b) Pairwise Jaccard similarity of per-example helped/hurt sets saturates at the first attention level (4\%) and remains flat.}
\label{fig:decoupling}
\end{figure}

Our analysis yields three findings. First, attention-driven convergence is task-specific. Of 10 benchmarks, only LAMBADA exhibits monotonic reduction in cross-architecture variance ($\rho = -1.0$, BCa 95\% CI $[-1.0, -0.7]$; cross-architecture $\sigma$ drops 79\%). LAMBADA \citep{paperno2016lambada} is a pure context-retrieval task that requires predicting the final word of a passage from long-range dependencies; attention provides direct token-level access to the full context, which offers a natural explanation for why convergence occurs on this task and not others. Benchmarks that involve reasoning or comprehension show no convergence, with all $|\rho| \leq 0.7$ and $p \geq .233$. The Bonferroni-adjusted $p = .100$ does not survive strict correction at $\alpha = 0.05$; we treat the confidence interval, which excludes zero, as primary evidence. Second, convergence depends on scale. At 1.3B parameters the $\sigma$ trajectory is monotonically decreasing, but at 340M it is non-monotonic: $\sigma$ partially rebounds at the highest attention ratio, with only 36\% net reduction versus 79\% at 1.3B. This contrast suggests that sufficient model capacity is a necessary condition for attention to fully equalize architectural differences on retrieval. Third, and central to our title-level claim, aggregate convergence and per-example divergence are decoupled. Cross-architecture $\sigma$ on LAMBADA decreases gradually across attention ratios ($0.024 \to 0.016 \to 0.014 \to 0.006 \to 0.005$), but pairwise Jaccard similarity of per-example helped and hurt sets saturates at the first attention level (4\%) and remains flat at approximately 0.095. Net asymmetry between architectures' per-example profiles grows from 0.013 to 0.040 as attention increases. These two quantities operate on different timescales: aggregate performance equalizes progressively, while per-example specificity is established from the earliest attention allocation and does not change with more attention.

Our contributions are as follows:
\begin{itemize}
    \item We quantify task-specific convergence: only retrieval tasks (LAMBADA) show attention-driven cross-architecture convergence at 1.3B scale; reasoning and comprehension benchmarks do not (\S\ref{ssec:task_convergence}, Table~\ref{tab:convergence}, Figure~\ref{fig:task_specificity}).
    \item We characterize scale-dependent convergence: the monotonic convergence pattern at 1.3B becomes non-monotonic at 340M, which suggests that sufficient scale is a necessary condition for complete equalization (\S\ref{ssec:scale_convergence}).
    \item We demonstrate that aggregate convergence and per-example divergence are decoupled: attention reduces cross-architecture variance gradually, but per-example overlap saturates at the first attention level and does not increase further, while inter-architecture asymmetry grows (\S\ref{ssec:decoupling}, Table~\ref{tab:per_example}, Figure~\ref{fig:decoupling}).
\end{itemize}

\section{Experiments}\label{sec:experiments}

We analyze how attention affects cross-architecture performance variance using models from the hybrid recurrent model zoo of \citet{wang2025hybrid}. Our analysis spans three architectures (GatedDeltaNet, RetNet, HGRN2), five attention ratios (0\% pure recurrent, 4\%, 7.7\%, 14.3\%, 25\%), and two scales (340M and 1.3B parameters). In the h$k$-1 notation used throughout, one attention layer is interleaved per every $k{+}1$ layers; h3-1 denotes 25\% attention. All models within each scale share identical training data and hyperparameters; they differ only in architecture and attention configuration. We evaluate on 10 benchmarks spanning retrieval, comprehension, reasoning, and mixed task categories, plus one supplementary benchmark (COPA, $n = 100$).

\subsection{Task-Specific Convergence}\label{ssec:task_convergence}

We first ask whether adding attention layers reduces performance differences between architectures, and whether any such reduction is uniform across tasks. For each benchmark, we compute the cross-architecture standard deviation ($\sigma$) at each attention ratio and measure the Spearman rank correlation ($\rho$) between attention ratio and $\sigma$ across the five configurations.

Table~\ref{tab:convergence} reports results for all benchmarks at 1.3B scale. LAMBADA is the only benchmark exhibiting monotonic convergence: $\rho = -1.0$ (exact permutation test, $p = .017$, two-tailed, $n = 5$, 120 permutations). Cross-architecture $\sigma$ drops from 0.024 to 0.005, a 79\% reduction. The 95\% BCa percentile bootstrap confidence interval for $\rho$ is $[-1.0, -0.7]$ (10{,}000 resamples), which excludes zero. After Bonferroni correction for six pre-registered benchmarks, the adjusted $p = .100$ does not survive strict Bonferroni correction at $\alpha = 0.05$. We therefore treat the confidence interval, rather than the corrected $p$-value, as the primary evidence for the LAMBADA convergence effect.

The task-specificity of this result is itself a finding. LAMBADA \citep{paperno2016lambada} is a pure context retrieval task: predicting the final word of a passage requires encoding long-range dependencies but no multi-step reasoning. Attention provides direct token-level access to the full context, so convergence on a retrieval task is consistent with attention compensating for differences in how recurrent architectures store and recall contextual information. The remaining benchmarks span comprehension (BoolQ), reasoning (HellaSwag, ARC-Challenge, WinoGrande, PIQA), and mixed demands (ARC-Easy, MMLU, SciQ, XStoryCloze). None shows significant convergence: all have $|\rho| \leq 0.7$ and $p \geq .233$. Two mixed benchmarks (ARC-Easy, MMLU) exhibit moderate negative correlations ($\rho = -0.7$) and high convergence ratios (7.70 and 2.27, respectively), but their non-monotonic trajectories prevent these correlations from reaching significance. The supplementary benchmark COPA shows a similar convergence ratio (5.57, $\rho = -0.67$) but is limited by its small sample size. For tasks that involve reasoning or comprehension beyond retrieval, architectural differences in recurrent layers remain performance-relevant regardless of attention allocation (Figure~\ref{fig:task_specificity}).

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig2_task_specificity.pdf}
\caption{Spearman $\rho$ between attention ratio and cross-architecture $\sigma$ for each benchmark at 1.3B scale. LAMBADA ($\rho = -1.0$; BCa 95\% CI $[-1.0, -0.7]$) is the only benchmark with a negative correlation whose confidence interval excludes zero. Benchmarks are grouped by task category.}
\label{fig:task_specificity}
\end{figure}

\begin{table*}[t]
\centering
\caption{Cross-architecture convergence across benchmarks (1.3B scale). $\rho$: Spearman correlation between attention ratio and cross-architecture $\sigma$ across five configurations. $p$: exact permutation test (two-tailed, 120 permutations). $\sigma$ values reported $\times 10^{2}$ (accuracy percentage points). Ratio: $\sigma_{\text{pure}} / \sigma_{\text{h3-1}}$. \textbf{Bold}: only benchmark with $\rho = -1.0$.}
\label{tab:convergence}
\begin{tabular}{llrrrrr}
\toprule
Benchmark & Category & $\rho$ & $p$ & $\sigma_{\text{pure}}$ & $\sigma_{\text{h3-1}}$ & Ratio \\
\midrule
\textbf{LAMBADA} & \textbf{Retrieval} & $\mathbf{-1.0}$ & \textbf{.017} & \textbf{2.41} & \textbf{0.49} & \textbf{4.87} \\
BoolQ & Comprehension & $0.2$ & .783 & 1.63 & 2.36 & 0.69 \\
ARC-C & Reasoning & $0.6$ & .350 & 0.96 & 1.32 & 0.72 \\
HellaSwag & Reasoning & $0.3$ & .683 & 1.17 & 1.18 & 0.99 \\
PIQA & Reasoning & $-0.5$ & .450 & 0.54 & 0.34 & 1.59 \\
WinoGrande & Reasoning & $0.1$ & .950 & 0.67 & 0.78 & 0.86 \\
ARC-E & Mixed & $-0.7$ & .233 & 1.00 & 0.13 & 7.70 \\
MMLU & Mixed & $-0.7$ & .233 & 0.92 & 0.41 & 2.27 \\
SciQ & Mixed & $0.1$ & .950 & 0.87 & 0.74 & 1.19 \\
XStoryCloze & Mixed & $-0.6$ & .350 & 0.82 & 0.55 & 1.49 \\
\midrule
COPA$^{\dagger}$ & Reasoning & $-0.67$ & .267 & 2.63 & 0.47 & 5.57 \\
\bottomrule
\multicolumn{7}{l}{\footnotesize $^{\dagger}$Supplementary ($n = 100$); excluded from multiple comparison correction.}
\end{tabular}
\end{table*}

\subsection{Scale-Dependent Convergence}\label{ssec:scale_convergence}

We compare the LAMBADA convergence pattern at two scales. At 1.3B parameters, convergence is monotonic: $\sigma$ decreases at every attention level from 0\% through 25\% ($\rho = -1.0$). At 340M, convergence is partial. $\sigma$ reaches a maximum reduction of 56\% at intermediate attention ratios, but rebounds at h3-1, yielding a net endpoint reduction of 36\%: the trajectory is non-monotonic, and the highest-attention model does not achieve the lowest cross-architecture variance. One possible interpretation is that at smaller scale, the capacity overhead of attention layers creates interference that offsets the retrieval benefit.

This contrast indicates that sufficient scale is a necessary condition for complete equalization on LAMBADA. We emphasize that this is a binary observation from two data points, not a scaling law. Characterizing the functional relationship between scale and convergence requires intermediate scales beyond the scope of this study. Despite the aggregate divergence, within-scale pairwise Jaccard similarity between architectures' per-example effects is nearly identical at both scales (helped: 0.094 at 340M vs.\ 0.096 at 1.3B; hurt: 0.090 at both). Per-example architecture specificity is stable across scales even when aggregate convergence trajectories differ, extending the decoupling observation from the attention-ratio dimension (\S\ref{ssec:decoupling}) to the scale dimension. Full cross-scale comparisons appear in Appendix~E.

\subsection{Per-Example Divergence}\label{ssec:per_example}

The aggregate convergence on LAMBADA raises a question: do architectures converge by improving on the same examples, or do they help and hurt different examples when attention is added? We compare each architecture's pure-recurrent baseline against its h3-1 hybrid variant on 5{,}153 LAMBADA examples, classifying each example as helped (correct only in hybrid), hurt (correct only in pure), or unchanged.

Table~\ref{tab:per_example} reports the results. Each architecture displays a distinct profile of per-example changes. GDN experiences a net loss of 153 examples (462 helped, 615 hurt), RetNet a net loss of 69 (426 helped, 495 hurt), and HGRN2 a net gain of 153 (548 helped, 395 hurt). The directional asymmetry is notable: HGRN2 is the only architecture for which attention produces a net benefit at the per-example level, while GDN and RetNet both lose more examples than they gain. Pairwise Jaccard similarity between any two architectures' affected sets is low: $J \leq 0.104$ for helped sets and $J \leq 0.096$ for hurt sets, both near the chance-level baseline for sets of this size. Each architecture is therefore affected on a largely non-overlapping subset of examples.

We tested whether surface-level features (token count, word frequency, syntactic complexity) predict which examples are helped or hurt within a given architecture. No feature reliably separates helped from hurt examples for any single architecture. A cross-architecture analysis, however, reveals structure in the divergence. A Kruskal-Wallis test on the hurt-example distributions across architectures is significant ($p = .0005$), and the corresponding test for helped examples also reaches significance ($p = .020$). The architecture-specific patterns of per-example effects are therefore structured rather than random, even though the source of this structure lies beyond simple surface features. This structured disagreement at the individual-example level, despite similar aggregate performance, connects to the underspecification phenomenon documented by \citet{damour2022underspecification}.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Per-example effects of attention (pure $\to$ h3-1) on 5{,}153 LAMBADA examples at 1.3B scale. $J_{\max}$: maximum pairwise Jaccard similarity with other architectures across helped and hurt sets.}
\label{tab:per_example}
\begin{tabular}{lrrrr}
\toprule
Architecture & Helped & Hurt & Net & $J_{\max}$ \\
\midrule
GDN & 462 & 615 & $-$153 & .096 \\
RetNet & 426 & 495 & $-$69 & .100 \\
HGRN2 & 548 & 395 & $+$153 & .104 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\subsection{Decoupling}\label{ssec:decoupling}

The central finding of this paper is that aggregate convergence and per-example divergence operate on different timescales as attention ratio increases (Figure~\ref{fig:decoupling}).

Cross-architecture $\sigma$ decreases gradually across the five attention ratios: $0.024 \to 0.016 \to 0.014 \to 0.006 \to 0.005$. Each additional attention layer provides incremental reduction. In contrast, pairwise Jaccard similarity between architectures' per-example effects saturates at the first attention level (4\% attention): approximately 0.095 for helped examples, with no further increase at higher ratios. Net asymmetry between architectures' per-example profiles increases from 0.013 at 4\% attention to 0.040 at 25\%.

These three quantities follow distinct trajectories. Aggregate variance requires progressively more attention to reduce. Per-example overlap reaches its ceiling with minimal attention. The asymmetry between architectures' per-example profiles grows as attention increases. This separation of timescales constitutes a decoupling: attention reduces the aggregate performance gap but does not homogenize per-example behavior. The per-example divergence in fact increases with attention ratio.

An analysis of attention weight patterns provides partial insight into this decoupling. At layer 2 of the 1.3B h3-1 models, GDN and RetNet produce nearly identical attention distributions (cosine similarity 0.995), yet their per-example effects differ substantially. HGRN2 attention patterns diverge from the other architectures at early layers (cosine $\sim$0.20) but converge at deeper layers ($>$0.94 at layer 20). Attention entropy does not differ between helped and hurt examples, which indicates that the per-example effects originate in the recurrent layers rather than in the attention mechanism itself. Full results appear in Appendix~D.

\subsection{Robustness}\label{ssec:robustness}

We test sensitivity to architecture inclusion by removing GDN and recomputing all correlations. The LAMBADA correlation weakens from $\rho = -1.0$ to $\rho = -0.7$ ($p = .233$), and MMLU reverses direction from $\rho = -0.7$ to $\rho = +0.6$. These shifts confirm that individual architectures influence the aggregate trends, consistent with the per-example divergence reported in \S\ref{ssec:per_example}. The 11.25\% degenerate bootstrap resamples in our BCa analysis arise from same-architecture triplicates that produce undefined $\rho$ (not $\rho = 0$); the percentile CI remains valid with 88.75\% informative resamples. Full sensitivity analyses appear in the appendix.

